{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>That you may enter into the covenant of HaShem your Elohim and into His oath.</b> This 'entering' (העברה) is an entry into the covenant, for it does not say לעברך על ברית but rather בברית, and this indicates that the intention is to enter into the covenant of the Holy One, blessed be He, just as one passes between the pieces to establish the covenant — not to transgress the covenant. From this Onkelos translated: לאעלותך בקיימא — 'to bring you into the covenant.' And as for what it says, ובאלתו — 'and into His oath,' this oath returns upon Israel, that is: with the oath that you imposed upon Him, by which He swore you in, and you accept it upon yourselves." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret ובאלתו as referring back to the Holy One, blessed be He, that is, 'and into the oath that He made for you' — meaning: you stand to enter into the covenant of HaShem and into the oath that He swore to you, that you shall be a people to Him and He shall be your Elohim. And the verse that follows it indicates this, for it says: כאשר דבר לך וכאשר נשבע לאבותיך — 'as He spoke to you and as He swore to your fathers.' And what strengthens this matter is that we find a full verse (I Samuel 12:22): כי הואיל ה׳ לעשות אתכם לו לעם — 'because HaShem was pleased to make you His people'; and although all the commentators, of blessed memory, interpreted it in the sense of desire and will, it is possible that this is included within it, and the primary sense is from the language of אלה — oath. And what further strengthens this for you is what they said in the Midrash on (Song of Songs 7:6) מלך אסור ברהטים — 'the king is bound by the strands': 'I have already sworn to your fathers that I shall never exchange you or your children forever, nor replace you with another nation.' And אסור is nothing but an oath, as it says (Numbers 30:11): ואסרה אסר על נפשה בשבועה. ברהטים — these are the fathers: Abraham, of whom it is written (Genesis 18:7): ואל הבקר רץ אברהם, and its Targum is רהט; Jacob, of whom it is written (Genesis 30:38): ברהטים בשקתות המים. Therefore He cannot transgress His oath. And so you find that when Israel sought to cast off the yoke of the kingdom of heaven from upon themselves in the days of Ezekiel, what is written there? (Ezekiel 20:1): באו אנשים מזקני ישראל לדרוש את ה׳. They said to Him: 'A priest who acquired a slave — may he eat terumah?' He said to them: 'Yes.' They said to Him: 'If he sold him, what then?' He said to them: 'He has already gone out from his domain.' They said to Him: 'We too have already gone out from His domain; let us become like the nations of the world.' He said to them: (ibid.) והעולה על רוחכם היו לא תהיה אשר אתם אומרים נהיה כגוים כמשפחות הארצות לשרת עץ ואבן, חי אני נאם ה׳ אלהים אם לא ביד חזקה וגו׳ ובחמה שפוכה אמלוך עליכם. He said to them: As long as he has not been sold with his consent, he remains his; and you were not sold for money, as it says (Isaiah 52:3): כה אמר ה׳ חנם נמכרתם ולא בכסף תגאלו."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:16.360237810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}